I think the problem is that the iron penalty would primarily affect combat
types*, whereas the spellcasting benefit of elven mithril would affect
primarily spellcasters.  Giving elves a leather helm might balance better;
it doesn't negate the effects of iron straight off, but it does patch
one of the armor slots that difficult to fill with non-iron items, and
it's useful to all classes.  By the time an elf is ready to wield something
beyond his/her starting (wooden) weapon, gloves should be obtainable.

* Not that there are many elvish combat types.  Ranger, I think,
and that's about it.

Perhaps elvish rangers could start with mithril armor, but it
should still work against magical abilities.  Spellcasters generally
don't wear metallic body armor at all, elvish or not, so it really
would be unbalancing to give them armor 2 points better than anything
else they can wear in that slot (apart from DSM) without hindering
casting at all at the beginning of the game.  

Of course, if we want to make elvish spellcasters really easy, this
could work.  Balance doesn't have to be respected.  Dwarvish valk vs.
human tourist or caveman, for instance.


